summary:
- topic: New Superintendent Welcome and Report
  text: Angela Atkinson Duina was welcomed as the new Superintendent, providing an
    initial report on district activities including security improvements, extended
    school year programs, and elementary reconfiguration work. She also highlighted
    collaborative sessions with district leadership focused on instructional leadership
    and the new equity policy.
- topic: FY2026 Budget Update
  text: The Finance Committee reported a more positive FY26 budget outlook than previously
    anticipated, attributing it to a successful spending freeze. However, final reconciliation,
    particularly regarding withholding accounts, is still pending, and the full financial
    picture will not be clear until the audit in October.
- topic: Student Cell Phone Policy 2026
  text: 'The board approved the second reading of Policy JICI: Cell Phones and Other
    Personal Electronic Devices in Schools, establishing a bell-to-bell ban. Detailed
    procedures for implementation are being developed by the administrative team and
    will be reviewed by the policy committee, with flexibility for schools to adapt
    specifics.'
- topic: Student Representative Input on Cell Phone Policy
  text: A statement from student representative Sarah Lian was read, outlining concerns
    about various cell phone policy options (pouches, lockers, classroom boxes) and
    advocating for an 'off and away' policy due to cost concerns and potential disruptions
    of other methods. She emphasized the importance of students learning to focus
    despite having their phones.
- topic: Elementary Reconfiguration Workshop
  text: A significant portion of the meeting was dedicated to a workshop on elementary
    school reconfiguration priorities, led by Assistant Superintendent Jo Prince.
    The workshop aimed to gather input from the community and board on balancing student
    stability, access, equity, and operational logistics, using data on demographics
    and academic outcomes.
- topic: Board Discussion on Reconfiguration Priorities
  text: Board members emphasized the need for equitable distribution of resources
    and student populations across schools, acknowledging the city's segregation and
    the school's role in fostering integration. Priorities included addressing socioeconomic
    equity, improving opportunities for grade-level collaboration among educators,
    and maximizing school facility use, while also considering the financial implications
    and learning from past integration efforts.
- topic: Public Comment on Budget and Reconfiguration
  text: Public comments highlighted concerns about the impact of the spending freeze
    on teachers, with one teacher citing instances of essential supply shortages.
    Community members also stressed the need for broader representation in reconfiguration
    discussions, particularly from immigrant communities and the west side, and emphasized
    the critical importance of school proximity for working families due to logistical
    and emotional strains.
